d,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ6When she had pierced and stricken through his temples.¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines“‚¤textÙ+At her feet he bowed, he fell, he lay down:¦layout ‚¤text¾At her feet he bowed, he fell:¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ(Where he bowed, there he fell down dead.¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines”‚¤textÙ,The mother of Sisera looked out at a window,¦layout ‚¤text¾And cried through the lattice,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ%Why is his chariot so long in coming?¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ%Why tarry the wheels of his chariots?¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤text½Her wise ladies answered her,¦layout ‚¤textÙ$Yea, she returned answer to herself,¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines•‚¤textÙ3Have they not sped? have they not divided the prey;¦layout ‚¤text½To every man a damsel or two;¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ#To Sisera a prey of divers colours,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ'A prey of divers colours of needlework,¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ^Of divers colours of needlework on both sides, meet for the necks of them that take the spoil?¦layout§quote_1ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses‘„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines“‚¤textÙ(So let all thine enemies perish, O LORD:¦layout ‚¤textÙJBut let them that love him be as the sun when he goeth forth in his might.¦layout§quote_1‚¤textÙ"And the land had rest forty years.¦layout­mid_paragraphªlast_verse